WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.003
 Doc, Brian, congratulations.

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:05.071
You fellas are in the last
round of this competition,

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:06.940
and now we're sending you
back to your home forges

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:13.846
to recreate this iconic
weapon set from history,

00:00:13.912 --> 00:00:17.116
the classic tomahawk and Bowie.

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:18.150
Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:20.519
We'll see you in four days.

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:21.521
 Good luck.

00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:29.195
DOC: Today, I have to build
a tomahawk and a Bowie knife.

00:00:29.261 --> 00:00:31.598
I have never made a
tomahawk, but I'm super

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:33.432
excited about doing that today.

00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:36.069
I'm planning on first
forging the Bowie

00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:39.705
and then start working on
the billet for the tomahawk.

00:00:39.772 --> 00:00:41.007
That went very well.

00:00:41.073 --> 00:00:42.808
Didn't have any major boo-boos.

00:00:42.875 --> 00:00:44.243
She is done for the night.

00:00:44.343 --> 00:00:46.645
Now it's time to start
working on the hawk.

00:00:46.745 --> 00:00:49.015
This is a wrought-iron leg vise.

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:52.151
I am going to salvage
some of the wrought iron

00:00:52.217 --> 00:00:55.020
and make the body of
the tomahawk with that.

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.757
Traditionally, hawks were
made with wrought iron

00:00:57.824 --> 00:00:59.158
with a bit welded into it.

00:00:59.224 --> 00:01:02.161
So I am going to do a
traditional tomahawk

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:03.896
with a bit insert.

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:05.064
Knife and a half a hawk.

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:08.568 line:7%
That's not too terrible.

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:12.204 line:0%
BRIAN: It's time now to
start on this tomahawk.

00:01:12.271 --> 00:01:15.241 line:0%
Drifting the eye, this is
uncharted territory, so we're

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:18.444 line:7%
going to see where we go.

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:21.614
I wanted this ax head to be nice
and tough, ready for the tests

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:22.916
they're going to do.

00:01:23.016 --> 00:01:26.653
So right now, I've got
my link from the shaft

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:28.021
to the cutting edge.

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:30.389
So at the end of day two,
I've got a knife that

00:01:30.489 --> 00:01:32.792
is ready to have the
handles on, and I've

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:37.096
got a tomahawk head ready
to be heat treated tomorrow.

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:40.833 line:0%
DOC: The morning of day
two, and I am stoked.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.335
Big plans for today.

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:44.703
With the tomahawk, I want
to go ahead and get the eye

00:01:44.803 --> 00:01:47.139
drifted, get the bit welded in.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.742
I have to get these
weapons heat treated today.

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:51.177
I think it's
looking pretty good.

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:54.180
Right now, I am going
to split the front of it

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:56.449
and get a bit in it.

00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:58.217
Put the cold bit
into the hot head.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.219
Get everything
fluxed up and start

00:02:00.285 --> 00:02:02.588
bringing it up to welding heat.

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:05.391
With the tomahawk, as
I'm drawing it out,

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:10.496
I noticed the dark
line, and it opens up.

00:02:10.562 --> 00:02:14.934
I'm going to get it hot and
hit it and see if that weld's

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:16.335
going to hold or not.

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:19.905
If this doesn't work,
then it'll be a whole lot

00:02:19.972 --> 00:02:21.407
of work today for nothing.

00:02:21.507 --> 00:02:23.542
It didn't weld to
the back of the bit.

00:02:23.609 --> 00:02:24.544
So--

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:25.545
It's done.

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:27.380
I've got to start
over from scratch.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:32.985
So I take a piece of leaf
spring, then start moving it.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.954
I am not happy today.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:37.656
I still have a lot
of stuff to get done.

00:02:37.756 --> 00:02:40.660 line:0%
I don't know if I'm going
to be able to pull it off.

00:02:40.726 --> 00:02:43.495 line:0%
BRIAN: Going into day three,
got my Bowie knife completed,

00:02:43.595 --> 00:02:46.532 line:0%
handle ready to put together,
and tomahawk ready for heat

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:47.300 line:7%
treat.

00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:56.809
I do my final test,
and she's a-skating.

00:02:56.875 --> 00:02:58.277
I love that sound.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:01.380
Got a pretty substantial
amount of black walnut here.

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:04.350
I have got to do
a lot of grinding.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:11.256
And I decide to
try to test fit it,

00:03:11.323 --> 00:03:13.860
and it just comes
right off of the end.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.228
I ground out too
much off the handle.

00:03:16.328 --> 00:03:18.030
Got a little grinder happy.

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:20.066
If I can't get this
fit up properly,

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:23.269
that could be a potential
place for failure.

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:25.605
The easiest thing
that I can do is

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:27.907
pop a little weld
inside this eye

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:31.377
and see if I can make this work.

00:03:31.443 --> 00:03:33.479
I get the tomahawk head slid up.

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:37.716
All that's left to do now is put
a few finishing touches on it.

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:40.686
 All right, bladesmiths,
welcome to the kill test.

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.955
We're going to find out
what kind of lethal damage

00:03:43.055 --> 00:03:44.590
your weapons will do.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:46.092
I'm going to take your weapons
and deliver some lethal blows

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:47.960
on this ballistics dummy.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:48.828
Doc, you're up first.

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:49.628
You ready for this?

00:03:49.695 --> 00:03:52.064
 Yes, I am.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:55.635
[music playing]

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:18.724
 All right, Doc, let's
talk about your set here.

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:22.862
I love the feel of your Bowie,
and I can thrust and slash

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:24.530
without any issues at all.

00:04:24.596 --> 00:04:27.066
Now your tomahawk,
good weight to it.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:28.867
You can definitely
feel an impact,

00:04:28.934 --> 00:04:32.138
and it definitely slashes nicely
when you're trying to cut.

00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:34.473
There is a little
rattle, but it's

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.210
not enough to where you're going
to feel like it's coming off.

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:41.447
Overall, sir, your tomahawk
and Bowie will kill.

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:42.314
 Nice.

00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:43.549
 All right, Brian,
your turn, sir.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:44.317
Are you ready?

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:45.050
 Let's do it.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.719
 All right.

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:50.690
[music playing]

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:12.344
All right, Brian,
talk about your Bowie.

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:13.813
The size is right
on your handle.

00:05:13.913 --> 00:05:16.249
The scale you have there
really fits in my hand.

00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:17.783
When you cut through
that and you stab,

00:05:17.883 --> 00:05:20.786
you can really feel
a deep wound channel.

00:05:20.886 --> 00:05:21.921
Now your tomahawk.

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:24.690
It's so light I'm like,
am I picking up a stick?

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:29.562
But when you strike this
skull, it dug in very deep.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:32.564
And overall, it will kill.

00:05:32.631 --> 00:05:33.799
 Yeah.

00:05:33.899 --> 00:05:35.435
 All right, gentlemen,
time for the strength test,

00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:36.736
our throw and chop.

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:38.604
I'll be using your
Bowies and tomahawks

00:05:38.704 --> 00:05:40.306
to throw into our
target wall and then

00:05:40.373 --> 00:05:41.974
chop into our fence rail.

00:05:42.074 --> 00:05:45.144
Now, this test isn't about what
your tomahawks do to the fence

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:46.546
rail or the target.

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:48.948
It's about what those targets
can do to your tomahawks

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:50.049
and/or Bowies, all right?

00:05:50.115 --> 00:05:51.116
All right, Doc, you're up first.

00:05:51.183 --> 00:05:51.883
You ready?

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:54.854
 Definitely.

00:05:54.921 --> 00:05:58.424
[music playing]

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:21.080
 All right, so, Doc, right off,
you got a good weight to it.

00:06:21.147 --> 00:06:23.649
There's enough weight there
to get some good momentum

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:24.550
in the throw.

00:06:24.616 --> 00:06:25.684
This is a friction fit, correct?

00:06:25.751 --> 00:06:26.685
 Correct.

00:06:26.785 --> 00:06:28.154
 It's a very traditional
way to do this.

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:29.689
I'm not surprised
that with throwing it,

00:06:29.755 --> 00:06:31.391
it's come a little bit loose.

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:33.493
As far as your Bowie goes,
the grinds are beautiful.

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:36.229
You've got that hollow, and
then your clip point meets it,

00:06:36.329 --> 00:06:38.330
and that gives a lot of
support for that tip.

00:06:38.431 --> 00:06:39.064
So well done.

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:40.132
 Thank you.

00:06:40.232 --> 00:06:41.000
 All right, Brian, you're up.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:41.900
Are you ready for this?

00:06:41.967 --> 00:06:42.768
 Yes, sir.

00:06:42.835 --> 00:06:46.139
[music playing]

00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:13.632
 Brian, right off,
that throws beautifully.

00:07:13.732 --> 00:07:15.868
There's a bit of a twisted
warp to the handle.

00:07:15.934 --> 00:07:18.237
So when I grab it and
index off the feel,

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:19.805
the head's just a
little bit to one side.

00:07:19.906 --> 00:07:22.474
As far as your Bowie goes,
I like the look of this.

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:24.377
Everything's still
tight as a set.

00:07:24.443 --> 00:07:25.977
I think they go
really well together.

00:07:26.044 --> 00:07:26.879
Nicely done.

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:28.147
 Thank you.

00:07:28.214 --> 00:07:30.483
 All right, bladesmiths,
this is the sharpness test,

00:07:30.549 --> 00:07:31.584
the sandman slice.

00:07:33.852 --> 00:07:35.655
Now, to test the
sharpness of your weapons,

00:07:35.755 --> 00:07:38.990
I will take your weapon
and cut up the sandman.

00:07:39.091 --> 00:07:40.125
What's up, Doc?

00:07:40.192 --> 00:07:40.859
You ready for this?

00:07:40.926 --> 00:07:41.827
 Definitely.

00:07:41.894 --> 00:07:45.731
[music playing]

00:08:09.755 --> 00:08:11.757
 All right, Doc, as you
can see with a compression

00:08:11.824 --> 00:08:16.662
fit, a couple of times
that thing loosened up.

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:18.530
It shouldn't be that loose
to where it comes and moves

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:19.931
around when you're
moving with the weapon,

00:08:20.032 --> 00:08:22.201
especially when you're
doing some kind of attack

00:08:22.301 --> 00:08:23.502
or a test with it.

00:08:23.569 --> 00:08:26.506
This is a nice stabber
with your Bowie here.

00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:29.808
And the chops, as you can
see, are also deep chops.

00:08:29.875 --> 00:08:31.977
But then again with
this kind of thing

00:08:32.044 --> 00:08:34.614
when you're chopping
through, it just loosens up,

00:08:34.714 --> 00:08:36.214
and that's an issue.

00:08:36.281 --> 00:08:38.851
Overall, it will cut.

00:08:38.918 --> 00:08:40.185
All right, Brian,
your turn, sir.

00:08:40.286 --> 00:08:41.086
Are you ready?

00:08:41.153 --> 00:08:41.787
 Yes, sir.

00:08:41.888 --> 00:08:42.822
Let's do this.

00:08:42.889 --> 00:08:46.192
[music playing]

00:08:57.002 --> 00:08:59.338
 Brian, let's talk about
your weapons right here.

00:08:59.405 --> 00:09:02.341
The Bowie's edge
slices and dices.

00:09:02.408 --> 00:09:03.943
You can hook nicely
with your tomahawk,

00:09:04.009 --> 00:09:06.712
and your tomahawk digs in
when you're cutting also.

00:09:06.812 --> 00:09:09.815
And overall, your
weapons, it will cut.

00:09:09.882 --> 00:09:11.584
 Thank you.

00:09:11.651 --> 00:09:13.785
 All right, gentlemen, your
weapons have been tested.

00:09:13.852 --> 00:09:15.988
The judges have
evaluated your work.

00:09:16.088 --> 00:09:21.092
Our new "Forged in
Fire" champion is Brian.

00:09:21.193 --> 00:09:22.628
Congratulations.

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:24.563
Unfortunately, Doc, at
this time I have to ask

00:09:24.663 --> 00:09:25.764
you to please leave the forge.

00:09:25.831 --> 00:09:27.966
Come on forward, my friend.

00:09:28.033 --> 00:09:30.970
 If I could have done
something differently,

00:09:31.036 --> 00:09:32.471
I would have probably
figured a way

00:09:32.571 --> 00:09:35.107
to place the pin
somewhere to keep the head

00:09:35.207 --> 00:09:37.176
from sliding back down.

00:09:37.276 --> 00:09:38.711
I came here to have fun.

00:09:38.811 --> 00:09:41.146
That was my number-one goal,
and this has been a blast.

00:09:41.213 --> 00:09:43.249
Probably been one of the
best experiences in my life.

00:09:45.918 --> 00:09:48.854
 Brian, you are the new
"Forged in Fire" champion,

00:09:48.921 --> 00:09:53.225
and that is a title that comes
with a check for $10,000.

00:09:53.325 --> 00:09:55.894
Congratulations, my friend.

00:09:55.961 --> 00:09:57.396
Come on over here
and shake our hands.

00:09:57.496 --> 00:09:59.130
Good job.

00:09:59.231 --> 00:10:00.032
 Thank you, sir.

00:10:00.099 --> 00:10:00.700
 Good job, man.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:01.567
BRIAN: It's amazing.

00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:02.868
I've learned a lot.

00:10:02.935 --> 00:10:04.903
I learned that you don't
quit when you're behind.

00:10:05.004 --> 00:10:07.773
Plans I have for that
$10,000 is taking my wife

00:10:07.839 --> 00:10:09.508
on the honeymoon we didn't get.

00:10:09.608 --> 00:10:11.510
We'll be 10 years
married, so I'm going

00:10:11.576 --> 00:10:12.544
to treat her to something nice.

00:10:12.645 --> 00:10:16.716
[music playing]

